<div class="item-row-area feature-itemlist">
    <div class="dragrow headlineNormal feature-itemlist" data-itemid="{{item._id}}">
        <div class="width-iconbox draggable"><i class="icon fa-solid fa-grip-vertical"></i></div>

        {{#if (eq locked false)}}
        {{#if (isChecked item.system.isactive)}}
            <i class="item-active pointer icon fa-regular fa-square-check" title="{{localize 'wod.power.active'}}" data-itemid="{{item._id}}" data-type="isactive" data-action="itemActive"></i>
        {{else}}
            <i class="item-active pointer icon fa-regular fa-square" title="{{localize 'wod.power.active'}}" data-itemid="{{item._id}}" data-type="isactive" data-action="itemActive"></i>
        {{/if}}
        {{/if}}

        
        {{#if item.system.isrollable}}
            <div class="largeBox vrollable" 
                data-rollitem="true" 
                data-itemid="{{item._id}}"
                title="{{item.name}}"
                data-action="rollDice">           
                {{#if (ne item.name "")}}
                    {{item.name}}
                {{else}}
                    {{localize unnamedtitle}}
                {{/if}}
            </div>
        {{else}}
            {{!-- `title` mirrors the rollable branch above. It is not decoration: the name box is
                  clipped to its column width in `css/wod.css` (".wod20 .item-row-area.feature-itemlist
                  .dragrow > .largeBox"), so a long name now ends in an ellipsis and the hover is the
                  only way to read the rest of it. --}}
            <div class="largeBox" title="{{item.name}}">
                {{#if (ne item.name "")}}
                    {{item.name}}
                {{else}}
                    {{localize unnamedtitle}}
                {{/if}}
            </div>
        {{/if}}

        {{#if showtype}}
        <div class="largeBox">{{localize item.system.type}}</div>
        {{/if}}

        {{#if item.system.isrollable}}
            <div class="width-namebox">{{shorten (localize (getAttributes item.system.dice1)) 10}}</div>
            <div class="width-abilitybox">{{shorten (localize (getAbility item.system.dice2 actor)) 10}}</div>
            <div class="width-valuebox">
                {{#if (eq item.system.difficulty "-1")}}
                    {{localize "wod.labels.varies"}}
                {{else}}
                    {{item.system.difficulty}}
                {{/if}}
            </div>
        {{else}}
            <div class="width-valuebox">
                {{!-- pointValue, not gt: a merit/flaw's point value can be the book's own
                      "1 a 5" / "1 o 3" / "1/2/3/4/5", which gt coerces to NaN and hides.

                      THREE candidates, tried in order, and BOTH extra ones are opt-in:
                        valuefirst    - read BEFORE `system.level`
                        system.level  - always, and the only candidate a caller cannot switch off
                        valuefallback - read AFTER `system.level`
                      `pointValue` skips a candidate that is null/undefined/""/0/negative, so a
                      caller that passes neither behaves exactly as before.

                      Neither is applied blanket-wise inside this partial, and that is not the same
                      choice `stats_feature_row.hbs` makes. This partial is shared by twelve blocks
                      across three tabs (merits, flaws, backgrounds, bloodbound, boons, oaths,
                      connections, headlines, other traits, apocalyptic forms, ...) and for several
                      of them `system.value` is not a point cost at all - a boon's `system.level` is
                      not even a number, it is a BoonTypes key. An opt-in keeps the blast radius at
                      exactly the callers that ask.

                      WHY TWO PARAMETERS AND NOT ONE. The three producers of a Feature disagree
                      about which field carries the number, and which of the two is authoritative
                      depends on the sub-kind:

                      - MERITS / FLAWS are POINT-COSTED. The compendium exporter writes the cost into
                        `system.level` and leaves `value` at 0 (measured: 411 merits + 436 flaws in
                        wod20-compendium-es, every one of them `value: 0`); the character exporter
                        hard-codes `level: '0'` and writes the cost into `system.value`. So `level`
                        wins and `value` is the fallback -> `valuefallback`.
                      - BACKGROUNDS, and the wraith Passion / Dark Passion / Fetter, are DOT-RATED.
                        `system.value`/`system.max` IS the rating: the Advantages block on the
                        Attributes tab renders it as an editable dot row, and the background roll
                        (`action-helpers.js`, `dataset.object == "wod.types.background"`) reads
                        `item.system.value` for the pool. No producer ever puts a Background's rating
                        in `level`: all 96 compendium backgrounds ship `level: '0'`, the character
                        exporter hard-codes `'0'`, and the system's own create button hard-codes
                        `level: 1` as a placeholder that never tracks the dots afterwards. So `value`
                        wins -> `valuefirst`. Passing `valuefallback` there instead would leave every
                        GM-created Background stuck printing that placeholder 1 over a real rating. --}}
                {{#if (pointValue valuefirst item.system.level valuefallback)}}
                    {{pointValue valuefirst item.system.level valuefallback}}
                {{else}}
                    &nbsp;
                {{/if}}
            </div>
        {{/if}}
    </div>
    <div class="iconBox">

        {{> "systems/worldofdarkness/templates/actor/parts/list_icons.hbs" showsendchat=true showedit=true showdelete=true showtooltip=true item=item locked=locked edittitle=edittitle deletetitle=deletetitle}}

    </div>	                
</div>
{{!-- The inline .description panel is removed: the eye above now opens the item's own sheet
      in a window (open-item-window-from-eye-icon). --}}